{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789407651,"nonce":"o9ZtEkd2iZtCYf3Uyep8Qro/yqc=","message":"2026-09-14: the isolate shape (1a4cd19) stays for whoever needs it;\nthe goal is this issue, Go-like M:N on one heap, so a Wren fiber and\nAsh's fiber-backed sys::thread share one mental model. Stages, each\nlanding on its own:\n\n1. Split VM into shared and per-thread: engine/code/tier state,\n   modules and module variables, classes, interner and the heap\n   behind an Arc\u003cShared\u003e; the current fiber, error state, pending\n   fiber action, JIT context and the scheduler world stay per\n   thread. Natives keep \u0026mut dyn NativeContext. No behaviour change.\n2. Multi-thread heap: per-thread allocation blocks from a shared\n   pool, stop-the-world at safepoints (every thread publishes its sp\n   and parks; the collector scans all thread and fiber stacks). The\n   epoch-word poll at compiled loop headers (c4d5a40) is a\n   prerequisite here.\n3. Shared state under Go's rules: one-word Values never tear; module\n   variables and fields race as Go's do, with a Mutex class for the\n   program. Memory safety where it must hold: List/Map mutation\n   (header spin-bit, measured against Ash parity first; else Go's\n   fatal \"concurrent writes\" detection), method-table growth and\n   code installs under the stop-the-world or atomic swaps, ICs are\n   single-word writes.\n4. Scheduler: a pool of worlds over 7776341's world-per-thread;\n   Fiber.go(fn) places a task on the least-loaded world, a running\n   krio fiber stays on its thread; channels enqueue the Value itself;\n   Mutex and WaitGroup. Fiber.spawn keeps meaning \"this world\".\n5. call/transfer/yield keep their meaning between fibers on one\n   world; only go introduces parallelism.\n\nEstimate 4–6 weeks with a soak on the corpus and hatch/site before it\nis the default. Starting with stage 1.","files":null}]}